  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Monday)\nIssued at 1202 AM CDT TUE SEP 29 2026\n\nThis evening, surface ridging is situated across Eastern Conus\nand centered over Ohio. In the upper levels, a weak ridge is\nacross Mexico into Texas pinned between a digging low over\nCalifornia into the Desert Southwest and a large trough out of\nNew York that extends down the East Coast with northerly flow\ninto Alabama. For today after a cool start, we are looking at a\nwarming trend to commence for the latter part of the work week.\nThe humidity will be low again on Tuesday, but our surface\npattern will start to change. By Wednesday, the surface ridge\ncenter will continue eastward over Virginia. This will create a\nsoutheast low level flow into Alabama with onshore moisture\nreturn and increasing dew points. In the upper levels for late\nTuesday into Wednesday, a bit of energy is expected to pull off\nfrom the main East Coast low as it pushes into the Atlantic and\nform a closed low over the Gulf Wednesday into Thursday. At the\nsame time, the upper ridge over Mexico will get pushed back to\nthe east by Wednesday for a short time over the Deep South as it\ngets wedged between the West Coast low opening up and moving ENE\ntoward the Central Plains and the low developing over the Gulf.\nMoisture will need to recover a bit before we can expect any\nsignificant rain chances. Very low rain chances return by\nThursday for portions of the area. More substantial chances hold\noff until this weekend ahead of an approaching front. There is a\nmedium chance of this front stalling/moving very slowly. This\nwill yield chances for some much needed rainfall Friday, over the\nweekend and continuing into the beginning of next week. At this\ntime, 2 to 3 inches of rainfall is likely across much of Central\nAlabama before all is said and done Friday through Monday. The\nhigher rain chances will also help to hamper the warm up from\nprogressing further over the weekend.\n\n08",
